          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            This article documents the differences between versions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CompLF+v0"} zero
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CompLF+v1"} one
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CompLF"} CompLF
          %h2#admissibility_vs_soundness Admissibility vs Soundness
          %p
            One of the most important differences between v0 and v1 is that many of the rules that are merely
            %em admissible
            in v0 are actually primitive or
            %em derivable
            in v1. In this regard, v1 is closer to Nuprl, and does not continue v0’s “experiment” with admissible rules
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CompLF#AdmissSem"} discussed previously
          %p The most conspicuous changes are the addition of some new rules in v1: the “sanity” or “presuppositionality” rule, and “inversion” rules for the type formation rules. Sanity is this one:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mo ⊩
                      %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash a \Vdash A}{\Gamma \vdash A\,type}
          %p
            There are (unfortunately) many inversion rules, but they all have the effect of saying merely that most of the type formation rules are invertible. For example, here are the inversions of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            formation:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:displaystyle => "true", :rowspacing => "1.0ex"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mfrac
                            %mrow
                              %mi Γ
                              %mo ⊢
                              %mi Π
                              %mi x
                              %mo :
                              %mi A
                              %mo .
                              %mi B
                              %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                              %mi type
                            %mrow
                              %mi Γ
                              %mo ⊢
                              %mi A
                              %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                              %mi type
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mfrac
                            %mrow
                              %mi Γ
                              %mo ⊢
                              %mi Π
                              %mi x
                              %mo :
                              %mi A
                              %mo .
                              %mi B
                              %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                              %mi type
                              %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                              %mi Γ
                              %mo ⊢
                              %mi a
                              %mo ⊩
                              %mi A
                            %mrow
                              %mi Γ
                              %mo ⊢
                              %mi B
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                              %mi a
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                              %mi x
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                              %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                              %mi type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{gathered}
                  \\frac{\Gamma \vdash \Pi x:A.B\,type}{\Gamma \vdash A\,type} \\
                  \\\
                  \\frac{\Gamma \vdash \Pi x:A.B\,type \qquad \Gamma \vdash a \Vdash A}
                  {\Gamma \vdash B[a/x]\,type}
                  \\end{gathered}
          %p
            Note that there’s a substitution baked into the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            codomain rule. This is because substitution is still an admissible rule. Not baking in a substitution here would probably mess that up.
          %p
            Less conspicuous, but just as important, are other changes throughout the rules of v0 that are possible because we no longer have to prove sanity and inversion by induction on formal derivations. Indeed, having sanity and inversion as primitive has the effect that many other rules become stronger: If you can derive
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⊩
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \Vdash A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and many of its subexpressions are all valid types. In v0, this was by carefully designing the rules so that all those extra consequences were already derivable. In v1, it’s actually revealing additional facts about the interpretation of the judgments. (Examples below.)
          %p Due to the use of PER semantics, to make the inversion rules sound, semantic equality of types is now intensional, like in Nuprl (and intensional type theory!), and unlike in v0. (TODO: Write an explanation of this.) As for sanity, that was actually sound all along, but including it as a primitive rule would ruin admissibility of inversion, were that not also primitive.
          %p Here is a list of changes to existing rules of v0 that are possible because sanity and inversion are primitive:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The direct computation rule, which lets you beta convert in a type, (and which previously used the auxiliary judgment (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ≡
                        %mi β
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \equiv_\beta
                ) but now uses (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi β
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \longrightarrow_\beta
                ), which is discussed
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#BetaConv"} separately
                %em must
                be valid, since it’s semantically the same as the old one, which is valid by assumption.
            %li
              %p As a consequence of the previous, the other old direct computation rule, which lets you beta convert in the subject, has become redundant and has been removed. To derive it, just switch the judgment to express membership with the equality type, use direct computation in that type, and switch back.
            %li
              %p
                The “select right” rule of equality, which goes from (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = a' \in A
                ) to (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ⊩
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a' \Vdash A
                ) has become redundant and has been removed. Prove symmetry in the usual way, and combine with select left. (Maybe it was already redundant before, who knows?)
            %li
              %p The subset elimination rule had a premise in v0 showing that the motive is a valid type family. In v1, this premise is removed. Semantically, it’s redundant because it follows from the typing premise for the sole case (and the additional free variable constraint).
            %li
              %p Similarly, the PER elimination rule had the motive validity premise removed.
            %li
              %p
                Yet another, the
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Bool
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool
                elimination rule had the motive validity premise removed. This one is a bit more interesting, because this rule has
                %em two
                cases, which only show validity of a substitution instance of the motive. Luckily, a family over the booleans only
                %em has
                two substitution instances, semantically.
            %li
              %p As a consequence of the previous, the rule saying booleans are computations has become redundant and has been removed. It follows from case analysis on the boolean, and the fact that both canonical booleans are obviously computations, by the computation formation rules. The reason this argument didn’t work in v0 is that you’d have trouble showing the motive is valid.
            %li
              %p
                Another consequence of the strengthened
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Bool
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool
                elimination which is perhaps much more surprising: The
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊥
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
                elimination rule, which effectively said that the two booleans aren’t equal, has become redundant and has been removed. Like in type theory with universes, you can prove this by interpreting the booleans as true and false
                = succeed "." do
                  %em types
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CompLF%2FHOAS#CombElim"} explained elsewhere
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Bool
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Bool
                elimination rule. (As icing on the cake, the derived
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊥
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bot
                elimination rule doesn’t need a motive validity premise either. But this seems to require
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#IdType"} identity
          %h2#relaxed_equality Relaxed Equality
          %p Another important change is the new equality formation rule. Here is the v0 rule:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi p
                      %mo ⊩
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≺
                      %mi C
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi q
                      %mo ⊩
                      %mi B
                      %mo ≺
                      %mi C
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mo ⊩
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi b
                      %mo ⊩
                      %mi B
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mo =
                      %mi b
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi C
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\Gamma \vdash p \Vdash A \prec C \qquad
                  \\Gamma \vdash q \Vdash B \prec C \qquad
                  \\Gamma \vdash a \Vdash A \qquad \Gamma \vdash b \Vdash B}
                  {\Gamma \vdash a = b \in C\,type}
          %p Here is the v1 rule:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ⊩
                      %mi Relax
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ⊩
                      %mi Relax
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                      %mo =
                      %mi a
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\Gamma \vdash a1 \Vdash Relax(A) \qquad \Gamma \vdash a2 \Vdash Relax(A)}
                  {\Gamma \vdash a1 = a2 \in A\,type}
          %p
            The new rule uses the new type constructor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Relax
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≺
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \prec
            %em not
            the main improvement. One could just make respect into a primitive type constructor to avoid the circularity.
          %p
            The real improvement is that “relaxed equality” avoids the implicit quantification over types in the v0 formation rule. Notice that the v0 rule involves the types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            which are not mentioned in the conclusion. Because of this, it was not clear at all how to give a strong inversion rule for equality formation. With relaxed equality, the inversion rules are rather obvious.
          %p
            Despite this major organizational improvement, the relaxed equality formation rule is saying essentially the same thing as the respect-based equality formation rule. This is because of a characterization of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Relax
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax
            in terms of subtyping: (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo &lt;
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                  %mo :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Relax
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \lt\!\!:\;Relax(B)
            ) if and only if (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ≺
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \prec B
            ). So some miscellaneous v0 rules about respect:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi p
                      %mo ⊩
                      %mi A
                      %mo ≺
                      %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi p
                      %mo ⊩
                      %mi Comp
                      %mo ≺
                      %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\Gamma \vdash A\,type}{\Gamma \vdash p \Vdash A \prec A}
                  \\qquad
                  \\frac{\Gamma \vdash A\,type}{\Gamma \vdash p \Vdash Comp \prec A}
          %p
            are replaced by v1 rules about
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Relax
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mo ⊩
                      %mi A
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi a
                      %mo ⊩
                      %mi Relax
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi t
                      %mo ⊩
                      %mi Comp
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi t
                      %mo ⊩
                      %mi Relax
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\Gamma \vdash a \Vdash A}{\Gamma \vdash a \Vdash Relax(A)}
                  \\qquad
                  \\frac{\Gamma \vdash A\,type \qquad \Gamma \vdash t \Vdash Comp}
                  {\Gamma \vdash t \Vdash Relax(A)}
          %p This allows the bootstrapping of general reasoning about respect to proceed in essentially the same way.
          %h3#greatest_type_respected Greatest type respected
          %p
            To understand how
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Relax
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax
            avoids quantification in the equality formation rule, it’s helpful to note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Relax
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax(A)
            is the greatest type (ordered by subtyping) that’s respected by
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Relax
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            are elements of
            %em some
            types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            that are respected by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            are elements of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Relax
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax(C)
          %h3#greatest_partition_thats_a_superset Greatest partition that’s a superset
          %p
            There’s another way to think about
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Relax
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subquotient"} subquotient
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Comp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Comp
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            in these ways?
          %p
            It turns out there’s a unique subset of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Comp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Comp
            of which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a quotient. It’s (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Comp
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Comp \cap A
            ), the type of computations that are also elements of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            It turns out that the other decomposition is
            %em not
            unique. But
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Relax
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax(A)
            is the greatest (ordered by subtyping) quotient of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Comp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Comp
            of which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a subset.
          %p
            Now here’s the cool part: Nuprl uses an equality formation rule that essentially uses the type (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∪
                  %mi Comp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cup Comp
            ) in place of our
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Relax
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax(A)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Base
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Base
            instead of
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Comp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Comp
            %em that
            type is the
            %em least
            quotient of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Comp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Comp
            of which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a subset!
          %p
            Indeed, (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∪
                  %mi Comp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cup Comp
            ) can be understood as the type you get from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            by adding all computations, that
            %em aren’t
            elements of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Relax
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax(A)
            instead adds
            %em at most one
            additional element, which is implemented by all computations that aren’t elements of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ∪
                    %mi Comp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cup Comp
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Relax
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax(A)
            both add all the remaining computations, but (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∪
                  %mi Comp
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cup Comp
            ) uses the finest possible equality, while
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Relax
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax(A)
            uses the coarsest.
          %h3#kleene_equality Kleene equality
          %p
            Thinking of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Relax
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax
            as adding at most one garbage element gives yet another way to think about it. The additional element can be regarded as an undefined element. It’s the element of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Relax
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax(A)
            whose realizers fail to denote an element of the desired type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Relax
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax
            is sort of like a lifted domain, and equality in a lifted domain is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kleene+equality"} Kleene equality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Relax
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax
            used in the semantics:
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Relax
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                  %mo ∨
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a = a' \in Relax(A))\;\coloneqq\;(a \in A \vee a' \in A) \to (a = a' \in A)
          %p
            From that definition, the general intro and elim rules for the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Relax
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax
            type constructor should be relatively clear.
          %p
            What this means for the equality type is that while it’s
            %em strict
            equality, when regarded as a relation on partial elements, it’s
            %em non-strict
            when regarded as a type-valued operation. That is, (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = a' \in A
            ) might be a valid type even if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and/or
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a'
            are not elements of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            (non-strict), but it cannot be
            %em true
            unless both
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a'
            are elements of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            (strict).
          %p
            Meanwhile, (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Relax
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = a' \in Relax(A)
            ) is non-strict in both senses, in terms of potential elements of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Relax
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax(A)
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Relax
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a = a' \in Relax(A)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a'
            are elements of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Relax
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Relax
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax(Relax(A))
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Relax
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax(A)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Relax
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax
            is idempotent. (You can’t add computations to a type that already has all of them.)
          %p
            All of this is provable internally, using the rules for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Relax
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax
            and equality.
          %h2#BetaConv Shortcut Beta Conversion
          %p
            To take a break from the big, important changes, here’s a minor, unimportant one. In v0, beta conversion was formulated as a judgment form (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \equiv_\beta
            ) which was defined to be the congruence closure of beta reduction. It turns out that that’s kind of a minor nuisance to spell out in a HOAS presentation of the rules. But because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            is the only binding form in the syntax, there’s a shortcut for getting full congruence. (In general, this shortcut does not avoid congruence rules for binding forms.)
          %p
            In v1, “partially-compatible beta reduction” (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \longrightarrow_\beta
            ) has only two congruence rules: one for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
          %p
            Obviously, consecutive applications of the direct computation rules allow rewriting with the reflexive, symmetric, transitive closure (equivalence closure) of (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \longrightarrow_\beta
            ). Let’s call that (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ↔
                    %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \leftrightarrow_\beta
            ). It turns out to be the same as (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \equiv_\beta
            ).
          %p
            What we seem to be missing is most of the congruence rules. But we have a trick: if (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mo ↔
                    %mi β
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a \leftrightarrow_\beta a'
            ), then
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %msub
                    %mo ⟵
                    %mi β
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mo ↔
                    %mi β
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi x
                  %mo .
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                  %msub
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi β
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  b[a/x] \longleftarrow_\beta
                  (\lambda x.b)\,a \leftrightarrow_\beta
                  (\lambda x.b)\,a' \longrightarrow_\beta
                  b[a'/x]
          %p by application argument congruence (extended to the equivalence closure) and the beta rule.
          %p
            So (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %msub
                    %mo ↔
                    %mi β
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b[a/x] \leftrightarrow_\beta b[a'/x]
            ). This lets us rewrite in any context
            %em if
            the hole does not appear under binders,
            %em or
            the thing we’re rewriting at least does not mention a bound variable. This actually seems to cover nearly all the cases of rewriting in practice, and then you don’t even need to bother with congruence rules, which is nice.
          %p
            But for the proof, what we need is that it means we effectively have congruence rules for all non-binding forms. And we really have the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            congruence rule for the only binding form in the language. So we effectively have all the congruence rules.
          %h2#letcomp Let-Comp
          %p Measured by utility, the most important change has got to be the addition of the “letcomp” rule:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "left", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi a
                          %mo ⊩
                          %mi A
                          %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi y
                          %mo :
                          %mi A
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi C
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %mi type
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Γ
                          %mo ,
                          %mi x
                          %mo :
                          %mi Comp
                          %mo ,
                          %mi x
                          %mo ′
                          %mo :
                          %mi Comp
                          %mo ,
                          %mi h
                          %mo :
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi x
                          %mo =
                          %mi x
                          %mo ′
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi A
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ⊢
                          %mi p
                          %mo ⊩
                          %mi c
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo =
                          %mi c
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi x
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %mi x
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mi y
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi x
                          %mo ,
                          %mi x
                          %mo ′
                          %mo ,
                          %mi h
                          %mo ∉
                          %mi FV
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi c
                          %mo ,
                          %mi c
                          %mo ′
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi q
                      %mo ⊩
                      %mi c
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo =
                      %mi c
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi y
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\begin{array}{l}\Gamma \vdash a \Vdash A \qquad
                  \\Gamma,y:A \vdash C\,type \\
                  \\Gamma,x:Comp,x':Comp,h:(x = x' \in A) \vdash
                  p \Vdash c[x/y] = c'[x'/y] \in C[x/y] \\
                  x,x',h \notin FV(c,c')\end{array}}
                  {\Gamma \vdash q \Vdash c[a/y] = c'[a/y] \in C[a/y]}
          %p
            In v0, quotienting was almost completely broken. It was
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CompLF#PERtheory"} explained
            how it was
            %em supposed
            to work, with parenthetical remarks that it didn’t yet seem to work. In v1, letcomp is what makes it work.
          %p Another solution presumably would’ve been to add a stronger elimination rule to PER comprehension. But this would not be taking full advantage of PER semantics, where quotienting is intuitively part of the very meaning of the judgments, and all types are implicitly subquotients.
          %p
            Letcomp is a subquotient elimination rule, formulated to work on an arbitrary type by regarding it as a subquotient of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Comp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Comp
          %p Letcomp can also be understood as internalizing the meaning of the semantic hypothetical judgment form:
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ≫
                  %mi c
                  %mo =
                  %mi c
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ↔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi type
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ≫
                  %mi C
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi type
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mi c
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x:A \gg c = c' \in C)\;\leftrightarrow\;((A\,type) \to ((x:A \gg C\,type) \wedge \forall a,a'.(a = a' \in A) \to (c[a/x] = c'[a'/x] \in C[a/x])))
          %p
            With this grand purpose as an attempt to complete the internalization of the PER semantics, it may not be surprising that letcomp is very useful for deriving rules for type constructors defined by PER comprehension. But it’s not perfect. Rules that involve metavariables without an associated typing premise tend to cause trouble. Alas, it seems very difficult to do much about that. (“Pullback” types à la
            %a{:href => "#MZFuncRefine"} Melliès &amp; Zeilberger
            might address the most serious gaps.) Notice however that intrinsic dependent type systems would not need rules with metavariables without typing premises. So the ability to derive type constructors and their rules seems quite substantial already.
          %p There was actually an early version of letcomp for v0. But the soundness of letcomp was not established until after changing the semantics to support primitive inversions.
          %h2#natural_numbers_type Natural Numbers Type
          %p
            CompLF v1 adds a type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Nat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Nat
            of
            = succeed "-encoded" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dana+Scott"} Scott
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peano+arithmetic"} Peano arithmetic
          %p
            The motive premise cannot be avoided in the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Nat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Nat
            elimination rule. One probable reason is that that would be too strong, since it would allow type-level recursion. But another reason has to do with technical details of PER semantics. (TODO: Explain.) Nuprl’s version of induction actually
            %em does
            avoid the motive premise. (Nuprl’s logic is much stronger than Peano arithmetic, by the way.) For some, this was a major advantage of Nuprl, since it’s a way to get nontrivial proofs of totality for recursive functions. But CompLF must pursue alternatives. (TODO: Explain!)
          %h2#IdType Identity Types
          %p
            Identity types provide another kind of equality type that also provides subsumptive rewriting. Unlike the main equality, which is type-directed, and only rewrites in well-typed goals, identity is untyped, and rewrites in any goal at all: rewriting has no motive premise. This identity should be thought of as a relation that’s true only for things that are identical, not a type of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+type"}> identifications
            ” of things that generally aren’t already identical.
          %p
            The restriction that prevents the two notions from collapsing is that identity types are usually not valid unless they’re true. Only when dealing with computations is it safe to assume identity. But in that case, it coincides with equality. In fact, identity
            %em is
            equality of computations, in the semantics. The different rules are due entirely to the difference in when the types are valid. This is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CompLF%2FHOAS#IdType"} explained elsewhere
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ≡
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \equiv t
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo =
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Comp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t = t \in Comp
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi Comp
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \in Comp
          %p When dealing with computations, where equality and identity coincide, the rules for identity types do indeed expose a stronger (derived) rewrite rule for equality. The motive premise is avoided:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi p
                      %mo ⊩
                      %mi t
                      %mo =
                      %mi t
                      %mo ′
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Comp
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi c
                      %mo ⊩
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi c
                      %mo ⊩
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi t
                      %mo ′
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\frac{\Gamma \vdash p \Vdash t = t' \in Comp \qquad
                  \\Gamma \vdash c \Vdash C[t/x]}
                  {\Gamma \vdash c \Vdash C[t'/x]}
          %p
            The identity type constructor
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#CompEqvITP13"} comes from Nuprl
          %h2#miscellaneous Miscellaneous
          %p
            Certain rules in v0 (equality formation (via respect) and PER formation) used non-dependent function types (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \to
            ) to express implications. In v1, non-dependent family intersection types (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊃
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \supset
            ) are used (in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Relax
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Relax
            introduction and PER formation) instead. In some cases, the propositions involved are guaranteed to be proof-irrelevant, so it doesn’t matter. In PER formation, there’s no guarantee that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is proof-irrelevant, but there’s no good reason for the user to use a proof-relevant relation, since the proof is ignored. In both versions of PER formation, you can run into trouble trying to use a proof-relevant relation. (So the change doesn’t help or hurt.)
          %p In v0, there was a reflexivity rule for equality, in addition to the uniqueness and selectivity rules. That was redundant. So now reflexivity is derived from uniqueness and select left.
          %p There’s been some reordering of rules, premises, and renaming of metavariables.
          %h2#references References
          %ul
